---
import Layout from "../../Base.astro";
import SimpleHero from "../../components/SimpleHero.astro";
import FeaturedArticles from "../../components/FeaturedArticles.astro";
import ServicesRelatedWork from "../../components/ServicesRelatedWork.astro";
import "../../styles/pages/_services-list.scss";
---

<Layout
  title="Social Media Content Strategy &amp; Production | Kelp"
  description="Kelp plans and creates social content that reflects your brand, explains your offer, and supports your wider marketing goals."
>
  <SimpleHero
    tagline="Content Creation Services"
    title="Social Media Content"
    description="Showing up on social is not the same as being remembered. We create platform-aware content that gives your brand a consistent story and a realistic publishing rhythm."
    lottieFile="social-media-content.json"
  />
  <div class="service-body">
    <div class="wrapper wrapper--narrow">
      <h2>Where teams get stuck</h2>
      <p>
        Most social channels do not stall because a business has nothing to say.
        They stall because production is inconsistent, the content is not built
        for the platform, or the team is constantly scrambling for the next
        post.
      </p>
      <p>
        That leads to a presence that feels reactive instead of intentional.
      </p>
      <h2>How we fix it</h2>
      <p>
        We build social content around the brand story, the platform, and the
        publishing rhythm your team can actually sustain. That usually means
        creating a healthy mix of formats instead of forcing every idea into the
        same template.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Infographics</li>
        <li>Data visualizations</li>
        <li>Microcontent</li>
        <li>Motion graphics</li>
        <li>Photography</li>
        <li>Video</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We also help identify which platforms deserve the effort so the work
        supports business goals rather than just filling a calendar.
      </p>
      <h2>A Content System Your Team Can Sustain</h2>
      <p>
        We define themes, formats, production needs, review roles, and a
        publishing rhythm. Depending on the goal, the mix may include project
        examples, product demonstrations, educational posts, photography, video,
        motion graphics, and campaign assets. We adapt the idea to the channel
        without letting the channel dictate the brand.
      </p>
      <h2>Connect Content to the Next Step</h2>
      <p>
        Each series should support a measurable purpose: awareness with the
        right audience, qualified site visits, event interest, enquiries, or
        customer education. Social reporting should inform the next production
        cycle rather than reward output alone. See Kelp's multi-channel work for
        <a href="/work/elev8-fun/">Elev8 Fun</a> or plan your content with us.
      </p>
      <h2>How This Works in Practice</h2>
      <p>
        A sustainable content plan makes room for the people and work that
        distinguish the business. We can build recurring themes from real
        customer questions, product education, behind-the-scenes process, and
        proof of completed work. Each format has a job: invite discovery,
        explain a point, answer an objection, or send someone to a relevant
        page. We review response and business relevance rather than posting
        volume alone.
      </p>
      <h2>What the Work Includes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Content themes and editorial calendar</li>
        <li>Copy, design, photography, or video production</li>
        <li>Channel-specific versions and review workflow</li>
        <li>Performance review for the next content cycle</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What Moving Forward Looks Like</h2>
      <p>
        Share recent posts, common customer questions, available footage or
        photography, and the people who can approve claims. We can build a
        sustainable series around what the business can genuinely show.
      </p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>How often should we post?</h3>
      <p>
        The right rhythm depends on production capacity and audience response.
        We would rather plan a repeatable series than set a frequency the team
        cannot maintain.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/contact/" class="button button--green">
          Plan Social Content
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <ServicesRelatedWork tag={72} />
  </div>
  <FeaturedArticles />
</Layout>
